  user_input {
    user_response: "/build android app named \"Create a complete native Android password manager named \342\200\234PW Manager\342\200\235 using Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, and Material Design 3.\n\nBuild a working Android Studio project, not a website, WebView, or visual-only prototype.\n\nPURPOSE\nAn entirely offline personal password vault that users can unlock with their phone\342\200\231s fingerprint or another supported strong biometric, with a master password as the recovery method.\n\nCOST AND PRIVACY\n- No Firebase, cloud storage, accounts, servers, paid APIs, ads, analytics, or subscriptions.\n- Do not add Gemini or other AI features.\n- Do not request INTERNET permission.\n- Store all vault data locally in app-private storage.\n- Never collect, access, or store fingerprint images or biometric templates. Use Android\342\200\231s system authentication interface.\n\nFIRST LAUNCH AND UNLOCK\n- Ask the user to create and confirm a strong master password.\n- Clearly explain that there is no \342\200\234forgot password\342\200\235 recovery service.\n- Offer biometric unlock after successful master-password setup.\n- Use AndroidX BiometricPrompt with BIOMETRIC_STRONG and a CryptoObject backed by an authentication-required Android Keystore key.\n- Biometric authentication must unlock cryptographic access to the vault; merely hiding the screen behind an authentication dialog is insufficient.\n- Check biometric availability and handle unsupported hardware, missing enrollment, cancellation, lockout, and invalidated keys.\n- If biometric access becomes invalid, require the master password and allow biometric setup again without losing the vault.\n- Never silently erase or recreate the vault after an unlock failure.\n- Allow master-password unlock when biometrics are unavailable.\n\nSECURE STORAGE\n- Generate a random vault encryption key using SecureRandom.\n- Encrypt all entry fields, including titles, usernames, passwords, website addresses, and notes, using authenticated AES-256-GCM encryption.\n- Generate a fresh unique nonce for every encryption operation.\n- Protect the vault key using two independent wrappers:\n  1. A key derived from the master password using a vetted password-based key derivation implementation.\n  2. An authentication-required Android Keystore key for biometric unlock.\n- Use a random salt and store versioned KDF parameters. Select and document a current, defensible KDF and work factor.\n- Never store the master password, derived keys, or decrypted vault key persistently.\n- Use established cryptographic libraries and Android APIs. Do not invent cryptographic algorithms.\n- If using Room, store encrypted records only; do not leave searchable plaintext metadata or plaintext database indexes.\n- Keep decrypted data in memory only while unlocked, clear references when locking, and document the limitations of memory clearing on Android.\n- Exclude vault files and sensitive settings from Android cloud backup and device-transfer backup rules.\n\nVAULT FEATURES\n- Add, view, edit, and delete entries.\n- Entry fields: title, username/email, password, website URL, category, and notes.\n- Categories: Personal, Work, Banking, Social, and Other.\n- Search decrypted entries locally while the vault is unlocked.\n- Favorites and alphabetical sorting.\n- Mask passwords by default, with a reveal/hide button.\n- Copy username or password with explicit user action.\n- Mark clipboard content as sensitive and clear it after approximately 30 seconds only if it still contains the value this app copied.\n- Generate passwords using SecureRandom, with configurable length and character options.\n- Confirm deletion.\n\nLOCKING AND SCREEN PRIVACY\n- Lock immediately when the screen turns off.\n- Default to locking when the app goes into the background.\n- Include a visible \342\200\234Lock now\342\200\235 action.\n- Prevent vault content from appearing in screenshots and recent-app previews using appropriate Android protections.\n- Never put secrets into logs, crash messages, notifications, saved UI state, or persistent navigation arguments.\n- Hide vault content immediately when the session expires or the process restarts.\n\nBACKUP AND RESTORE\n- Support manual encrypted backup and restore through Android\342\200\231s system document picker.\n- Require a separate backup passphrase and confirmation.\n- Use a versioned authenticated encrypted backup format with random salt, stored KDF parameters, and fresh nonces.\n- The backup must restore on another device without relying on the original device\342\200\231s Keystore.\n- Never offer plaintext CSV export.\n- Validate and decrypt imports before modifying the existing vault.\n- Explain that uninstalling the app or losing the phone without an encrypted backup can permanently lose the data.\n- Warn that forgetting the backup passphrase makes the backup unusable.\n\nDESIGN\n- Modern Material 3 UI with large readable text.\n- Deep navy and teal colors, with Light, Dark, and System themes.\n- Top app bar: vault icon and \342\200\234PocketVault\342\200\235 on the left; lock and settings actions on the right.\n- Search field, category filters, and a clean password-entry list.\n- Floating \342\200\234+\342\200\235 button for adding entries.\n- No bottom navigation.\n- Attractive empty states and accessible touch targets.\n- Settings: theme, biometric unlock, change master password, encrypted backup, restore, and privacy information.\n- Changing the master password must securely rewrap the vault key and preserve all entries.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION AND VALIDATION\n- Use ViewModel, StateFlow, coroutines, and clear separation between UI, storage, and cryptography.\n- Use maintained dependencies and explain their licenses and any limitations.\n- Handle interrupted writes safely to prevent vault corruption.\n- Do not include sample real credentials, hardcoded encryption keys, authentication bypasses, or unfinished security placeholders.\n- Test wrong-password rejection, tampered-ciphertext rejection, nonce uniqueness, lock lifecycle, biometric-key invalidation recovery, and encrypted backup restoration.\n- Explain how to test biometrics on an emulator and a physical phone.\n- Provide build instructions and a concise security-design explanation.\n- Clearly identify any security requirements that could not be implemented or verified.\n- Do not claim the app is audited, unhackable, or ready for sensitive production use without independent review.\n\nDeliver the complete functioning project.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      item {
        text_block {
          content: "---\ntype: \"feature\"\ntitle: \"Build an Android app\"\nicon: \"android_color\"\n---\n\n"
        }
      }
    }
    items {
      text: "/build android app named \"Create a complete native Android password manager named \342\200\234PW Manager\342\200\235 using Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, and Material Design 3.\n\nBuild a working Android Studio project, not a website, WebView, or visual-only prototype.\n\nPURPOSE\nAn entirely offline personal password vault that users can unlock with their phone\342\200\231s fingerprint or another supported strong biometric, with a master password as the recovery method.\n\nCOST AND PRIVACY\n- No Firebase, cloud storage, accounts, servers, paid APIs, ads, analytics, or subscriptions.\n- Do not add Gemini or other AI features.\n- Do not request INTERNET permission.\n- Store all vault data locally in app-private storage.\n- Never collect, access, or store fingerprint images or biometric templates. Use Android\342\200\231s system authentication interface.\n\nFIRST LAUNCH AND UNLOCK\n- Ask the user to create and confirm a strong master password.\n- Clearly explain that there is no \342\200\234forgot password\342\200\235 recovery service.\n- Offer biometric unlock after successful master-password setup.\n- Use AndroidX BiometricPrompt with BIOMETRIC_STRONG and a CryptoObject backed by an authentication-required Android Keystore key.\n- Biometric authentication must unlock cryptographic access to the vault; merely hiding the screen behind an authentication dialog is insufficient.\n- Check biometric availability and handle unsupported hardware, missing enrollment, cancellation, lockout, and invalidated keys.\n- If biometric access becomes invalid, require the master password and allow biometric setup again without losing the vault.\n- Never silently erase or recreate the vault after an unlock failure.\n- Allow master-password unlock when biometrics are unavailable.\n\nSECURE STORAGE\n- Generate a random vault encryption key using SecureRandom.\n- Encrypt all entry fields, including titles, usernames, passwords, website addresses, and notes, using authenticated AES-256-GCM encryption.\n- Generate a fresh unique nonce for every encryption operation.\n- Protect the vault key using two independent wrappers:\n  1. A key derived from the master password using a vetted password-based key derivation implementation.\n  2. An authentication-required Android Keystore key for biometric unlock.\n- Use a random salt and store versioned KDF parameters. Select and document a current, defensible KDF and work factor.\n- Never store the master password, derived keys, or decrypted vault key persistently.\n- Use established cryptographic libraries and Android APIs. Do not invent cryptographic algorithms.\n- If using Room, store encrypted records only; do not leave searchable plaintext metadata or plaintext database indexes.\n- Keep decrypted data in memory only while unlocked, clear references when locking, and document the limitations of memory clearing on Android.\n- Exclude vault files and sensitive settings from Android cloud backup and device-transfer backup rules.\n\nVAULT FEATURES\n- Add, view, edit, and delete entries.\n- Entry fields: title, username/email, password, website URL, category, and notes.\n- Categories: Personal, Work, Banking, Social, and Other.\n- Search decrypted entries locally while the vault is unlocked.\n- Favorites and alphabetical sorting.\n- Mask passwords by default, with a reveal/hide button.\n- Copy username or password with explicit user action.\n- Mark clipboard content as sensitive and clear it after approximately 30 seconds only if it still contains the value this app copied.\n- Generate passwords using SecureRandom, with configurable length and character options.\n- Confirm deletion.\n\nLOCKING AND SCREEN PRIVACY\n- Lock immediately when the screen turns off.\n- Default to locking when the app goes into the background.\n- Include a visible \342\200\234Lock now\342\200\235 action.\n- Prevent vault content from appearing in screenshots and recent-app previews using appropriate Android protections.\n- Never put secrets into logs, crash messages, notifications, saved UI state, or persistent navigation arguments.\n- Hide vault content immediately when the session expires or the process restarts.\n\nBACKUP AND RESTORE\n- Support manual encrypted backup and restore through Android\342\200\231s system document picker.\n- Require a separate backup passphrase and confirmation.\n- Use a versioned authenticated encrypted backup format with random salt, stored KDF parameters, and fresh nonces.\n- The backup must restore on another device without relying on the original device\342\200\231s Keystore.\n- Never offer plaintext CSV export.\n- Validate and decrypt imports before modifying the existing vault.\n- Explain that uninstalling the app or losing the phone without an encrypted backup can permanently lose the data.\n- Warn that forgetting the backup passphrase makes the backup unusable.\n\nDESIGN\n- Modern Material 3 UI with large readable text.\n- Deep navy and teal colors, with Light, Dark, and System themes.\n- Top app bar: vault icon and \342\200\234PocketVault\342\200\235 on the left; lock and settings actions on the right.\n- Search field, category filters, and a clean password-entry list.\n- Floating \342\200\234+\342\200\235 button for adding entries.\n- No bottom navigation.\n- Attractive empty states and accessible touch targets.\n- Settings: theme, biometric unlock, change master password, encrypted backup, restore, and privacy information.\n- Changing the master password must securely rewrap the vault key and preserve all entries.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION AND VALIDATION\n- Use ViewModel, StateFlow, coroutines, and clear separation between UI, storage, and cryptography.\n- Use maintained dependencies and explain their licenses and any limitations.\n- Handle interrupted writes safely to prevent vault corruption.\n- Do not include sample real credentials, hardcoded encryption keys, authentication bypasses, or unfinished security placeholders.\n- Test wrong-password rejection, tampered-ciphertext rejection, nonce uniqueness, lock lifecycle, biometric-key invalidation recovery, and encrypted backup restoration.\n- Explain how to test biometrics on an emulator and a physical phone.\n- Provide build instructions and a concise security-design explanation.\n- Clearly identify any security requirements that could not be implemented or verified.\n- Do not claim the app is audited, unhackable, or ready for sensitive production use without independent review.\n\nDeliver the complete functioning project.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nInstructions: If you need more context from the user you must first ask the user some questions using the ask_question tool. If you ask questions, you must wait for the user\'s answers. After the questions you must present a plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    }
  }
}
